<!doctype html>
<html>
  <head>
    <title>XD</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <%#
      This page hands the OAuth response to the page that opened the popup or framed the hidden
      iframe.  Two things here used to be wrong, and both mattered:

        * the target origin was "*", so the message went to whatever had opened the window.
          Because redirect_url_valid? waves through any request with display=popup or
          display=hidden, that was enough for any site to open the authorize endpoint and
          collect a logged-in user's access and refresh tokens.  It is now the application's own
          registered origin -- see Platform::OauthHardening#xd_target_origin -- so the browser
          refuses to deliver the message to anyone else.

        * the payload was the entire params hash, so anything the request carried came back out
          in a message the receiving page dispatches on.  It is now only the response
          parameters -- see #xd_response_payload.

      The payload stays a query string, not an object: Platform.Request.onMessage in jsdk.js only
      parses event.data when it is a string, and third parties host their own copies of that
      file, so the wire format cannot change without breaking integrations we cannot update.
      The receiving half of the origin check lives in that same function.
    -%>
    <% javascript_tag do %>
      var PLATFORM_XD_PAYLOAD       = <%= @xd_payload_query.to_json %>;
      var PLATFORM_XD_TARGET_ORIGIN = <%= @xd_post_target.to_json %>;

      function sendFragment() {
        var target = (window.opener || window.parent || window.top);
        if (!target || target === window) return;

        target.postMessage(PLATFORM_XD_PAYLOAD, PLATFORM_XD_TARGET_ORIGIN);
      }

      document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", sendFragment);
    <% end %>
  </body>
</html>
